      "id": 30417,
      "name": "Vyolynce",
      "username": "Vyolynce",
      "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/vyolynce/{size}/2397_2.png",
      "created_at": "2012-05-13T22:04:34.000Z",
      "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote no-group\" data-username=\"&quot;&quot;kz3rt&quot;&quot;\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n \"\"kz3rt\"\":</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p><a class=\"mention\" href=\"/u/josul0215\">@josul0215</a>: Actually, it is; if a creatures toughness is reduced to zero, it’s placed in the graveyard as a state-based effect, such that regeneration and the like will not trigger on its demise. It’s not destroyed, like it would be from combat damage (that isn’t from a creature with infect) or by (most) spells.</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>It may not be “destroyed”, but it does “die,” to use the current rules term. If SotM worked like MTG (and in a lot of ways it does not, but this one lines up), she (and the Carrion Fields) would most definitely see the 0HP birds die and trigger accordingly.</p>",
